1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,845
Hi, I'm Ryan Rover.

2
00:00:02,025 --> 00:00:03,621
We've all been told that the way

3
00:00:03,621 --> 00:00:05,067
to get rich is to save money.

4
00:00:05,247 --> 00:00:06,237
Skip the coffee,

5
00:00:06,237 --> 00:00:07,289
clip the coupons,

6
00:00:07,289 --> 00:00:09,206
put something away every month.

7
00:00:09,386 --> 00:00:10,930
And saving matters,

8
00:00:10,930 --> 00:00:11,418
a lot.

9
00:00:11,598 --> 00:00:13,607
But here's the uncomfortable truth.

10
00:00:13,787 --> 00:00:16,105
Saving alone, money sitting in a bank

11
00:00:16,105 --> 00:00:18,674
account, will almost never make you rich.

12
00:00:18,854 --> 00:00:20,372
In fact, over decades,

13
00:00:20,372 --> 00:00:22,512
it can quietly make you poorer.

14
00:00:22,692 --> 00:00:24,236
Today I'll show you why,

15
00:00:24,236 --> 00:00:27,068
and what actually turns savings into wealth.

16
00:00:27,419 --> 00:00:29,323
Here's the math in one picture.

17
00:00:29,503 --> 00:00:31,330
Five hundred dollars a month,

18
00:00:31,330 --> 00:00:33,725
kept in cash earning nothing for forty

19
00:00:33,725 --> 00:00:35,931
years, adds up to two hundred forty

20
00:00:35,931 --> 00:00:37,003
thousand dollars.

21
00:00:37,183 --> 00:00:39,654
The same five hundred dollars a month,

22
00:00:39,654 --> 00:00:42,386
invested at a seven percent average yearly

23
00:00:42,386 --> 00:00:44,923
return, could grow to roughly one point

24
00:00:44,923 --> 00:00:46,354
three million dollars.

25
00:00:46,534 --> 00:00:47,533
Same habit.

26
00:00:47,713 --> 00:00:48,514
Same effort.

27
00:00:48,694 --> 00:00:50,679
More than five times the result.

28
00:00:51,030 --> 00:00:53,540
And that comparison is generous to cash,

29
00:00:53,540 --> 00:00:55,360
because it ignores inflation.

30
00:00:55,540 --> 00:00:56,579
Over forty years,

31
00:00:56,579 --> 00:00:59,023
rising prices would shrink what that two

32
00:00:59,023 --> 00:01:01,956
hundred forty thousand dollars can actually buy,

33
00:01:01,956 --> 00:01:03,667
probably to well under half.

34
00:01:03,847 --> 00:01:05,345
Saving builds the pile.

35
00:01:05,525 --> 00:01:06,790
But without growth,

36
00:01:06,790 --> 00:01:09,186
the pile is slowly melting while you

37
00:01:09,186 --> 00:01:09,786
build it.

38
00:01:09,966 --> 00:01:12,485
That's the core idea of this video.

39
00:01:12,665 --> 00:01:14,611
Saving is the start of wealth,

40
00:01:14,611 --> 00:01:15,974
not the whole recipe.

41
00:01:16,324 --> 00:01:18,727
We'll explore this from three angles.

42
00:01:18,907 --> 00:01:20,346
First, the numbers.

43
00:01:20,526 --> 00:01:23,564
Inflation, interest, taxes and the ceiling on

44
00:01:23,564 --> 00:01:25,252
how much anyone can save.

45
00:01:25,432 --> 00:01:27,347
Second, the psychology.

46
00:01:27,527 --> 00:01:29,282
Why saving feels so safe,

47
00:01:29,282 --> 00:01:31,809
and why that feeling can hold people

48
00:01:31,809 --> 00:01:32,160
back.

49
00:01:32,340 --> 00:01:33,907
And third, the playbook.

50
00:01:34,087 --> 00:01:36,659
The three things that actually build wealth

51
00:01:36,659 --> 00:01:38,693
when you combine them with saving,

52
00:01:38,693 --> 00:01:40,786
investing, earning more and owning.

53
00:01:40,966 --> 00:01:43,157
Reminder, this is education,

54
00:01:43,157 --> 00:01:44,801
not financial advice,

55
00:01:44,801 --> 00:01:48,087
and all growth examples are illustrations,

56
00:01:48,087 --> 00:01:49,105
not promises.

57
00:01:49,455 --> 00:01:51,045
Let's start with the numbers.

58
00:01:51,225 --> 00:01:53,211
And the first lesson is about the

59
00:01:53,211 --> 00:01:55,498
silent thief in every savings account.

60
00:01:55,848 --> 00:01:56,811
Our numbers.

61
00:01:56,991 --> 00:01:59,530
Inflation data comes from the Bureau of

62
00:01:59,530 --> 00:02:00,637
Labor Statistics.

63
00:02:00,817 --> 00:02:02,809
Long run returns on stocks,

64
00:02:02,809 --> 00:02:05,614
bonds and cash come from research like

65
00:02:05,614 --> 00:02:08,492
Jeremy Siegel's Stocks for the Long Run

66
00:02:08,492 --> 00:02:10,337
and data from S and P Dow

67
00:02:10,337 --> 00:02:11,370
Jones Indices.

68
00:02:11,550 --> 00:02:13,768
Account rules come from the IRS.

69
00:02:13,948 --> 00:02:17,263
Growth examples use monthly compounding at stated

70
00:02:17,263 --> 00:02:19,564
average returns, as illustrations,

71
00:02:19,564 --> 00:02:20,647
not predictions.

72
00:02:20,997 --> 00:02:21,705
Angle one.

73
00:02:21,885 --> 00:02:22,732
The numbers.

74
00:02:22,912 --> 00:02:25,286
Seven reasons the math of saving alone

75
00:02:25,286 --> 00:02:26,848
doesn't add up to wealth.

76
00:02:27,198 --> 00:02:28,405
Lesson number one.

77
00:02:28,585 --> 00:02:30,965
Inflation slowly eats cash.

78
00:02:31,315 --> 00:02:33,989
Inflation means prices rise over time,

79
00:02:33,989 --> 00:02:36,311
so each dollar buys a little less

80
00:02:36,311 --> 00:02:37,085
every year.

81
00:02:37,265 --> 00:02:38,591
In the United States,

82
00:02:38,591 --> 00:02:41,497
inflation has averaged roughly three percent a

83
00:02:41,497 --> 00:02:42,950
year over the long run,

84
00:02:42,950 --> 00:02:45,792
according to Bureau of Labor Statistics data,

85
00:02:45,792 --> 00:02:48,445
though some periods have been much higher.

86
00:02:48,625 --> 00:02:52,073
In 2022, it rose above eight percent.

87
00:02:52,424 --> 00:02:54,397
Here's what that does to savings.

88
00:02:54,577 --> 00:02:57,026
Ten thousand dollars in an account earning

89
00:02:57,026 --> 00:02:57,901
half a percent,

90
00:02:57,901 --> 00:02:59,767
with inflation at three percent,

91
00:02:59,767 --> 00:03:02,041
would buy only about what four thousand

92
00:03:02,041 --> 00:03:04,665
eight hundred dollars buys today after thirty

93
00:03:04,665 --> 00:03:05,015
years.

94
00:03:05,195 --> 00:03:07,602
The balance grew a little on paper,

95
00:03:07,602 --> 00:03:09,596
but its real value was cut by

96
00:03:09,596 --> 00:03:10,628
more than half.

97
00:03:10,978 --> 00:03:13,944
That's why economists talk about real returns,

98
00:03:13,944 --> 00:03:15,749
your return after inflation.

99
00:03:15,929 --> 00:03:18,677
If your savings earn less than inflation,

100
00:03:18,677 --> 00:03:20,620
your real return is negative.

101
00:03:20,800 --> 00:03:23,017
You're losing buying power every year,

102
00:03:23,017 --> 00:03:25,409
even though your balance never goes down.

103
00:03:25,759 --> 00:03:27,717
High yield savings accounts help,

104
00:03:27,717 --> 00:03:30,507
and sometimes they've paid more than inflation.

105
00:03:30,687 --> 00:03:32,220
But over long periods,

106
00:03:32,220 --> 00:03:35,076
cash has generally only roughly kept pace

107
00:03:35,076 --> 00:03:36,678
with inflation at best.

108
00:03:36,858 --> 00:03:38,042
It preserves money.

109
00:03:38,222 --> 00:03:40,289
It rarely grows it in a meaningful

110
00:03:40,289 --> 00:03:40,532
way.

111
00:03:40,883 --> 00:03:42,701
Think about what things cost when your

112
00:03:42,701 --> 00:03:43,611
parents were young.

113
00:03:43,791 --> 00:03:45,083
A movie ticket,

114
00:03:45,083 --> 00:03:46,548
a gallon of milk,

115
00:03:46,548 --> 00:03:47,409
a new car,

116
00:03:47,409 --> 00:03:48,098
a house.

117
00:03:48,278 --> 00:03:50,789
Prices that once seemed normal now look

118
00:03:50,789 --> 00:03:51,111
tiny.

119
00:03:51,291 --> 00:03:54,507
That's inflation working slowly over decades.

120
00:03:54,687 --> 00:03:56,793
Your savings face the same force,

121
00:03:56,793 --> 00:03:58,898
which is why money that must last

122
00:03:58,898 --> 00:04:01,195
thirty or forty years needs to grow,

123
00:04:01,195 --> 00:04:02,024
not just sit.

124
00:04:02,375 --> 00:04:03,199
Try this.

125
00:04:03,379 --> 00:04:05,148
Look at the interest rate on your

126
00:04:05,148 --> 00:04:07,238
savings account and compare it with the

127
00:04:07,238 --> 00:04:08,418
latest inflation rate.

128
00:04:08,598 --> 00:04:09,988
If inflation is higher,

129
00:04:09,988 --> 00:04:12,405
your savings are shrinking in real terms

130
00:04:12,405 --> 00:04:13,009
right now.

131
00:04:13,360 --> 00:04:14,462
Lesson number two.

132
00:04:14,642 --> 00:04:17,290
The growth gap between cash and investing.

133
00:04:17,640 --> 00:04:20,136
Let's go deeper into the opening example.

134
00:04:20,316 --> 00:04:22,557
Five hundred dollars a month for forty

135
00:04:22,557 --> 00:04:23,914
years, earning nothing,

136
00:04:23,914 --> 00:04:26,156
is two hundred forty thousand dollars.

137
00:04:26,336 --> 00:04:28,538
Earning half a percent in a typical

138
00:04:28,538 --> 00:04:31,496
savings account, it's roughly two hundred sixty

139
00:04:31,496 --> 00:04:32,315
six thousand.

140
00:04:32,495 --> 00:04:34,405
Invested at seven percent,

141
00:04:34,405 --> 00:04:36,756
roughly one point three million.

142
00:04:37,106 --> 00:04:38,348
Why such a huge gap?

143
00:04:38,528 --> 00:04:41,565
Because compounding needs a meaningful rate to

144
00:04:41,565 --> 00:04:41,895
work.

145
00:04:42,075 --> 00:04:43,360
At half a percent,

146
00:04:43,360 --> 00:04:45,360
your money barely snowballs.

147
00:04:45,540 --> 00:04:46,685
At seven percent,

148
00:04:46,685 --> 00:04:49,312
growth starts producing its own growth,

149
00:04:49,312 --> 00:04:50,458
and over decades,

150
00:04:50,458 --> 00:04:53,017
that growth becomes most of the total.

151
00:04:53,368 --> 00:04:56,787
Historically, long term research like Jeremy Siegel's

152
00:04:56,787 --> 00:04:57,884
has found that U.

153
00:04:58,064 --> 00:04:58,412
S.

154
00:04:58,592 --> 00:05:01,502
stocks returned roughly six to seven percent

155
00:05:01,502 --> 00:05:03,949
a year after inflation over very long

156
00:05:03,949 --> 00:05:07,321
periods, while short term government bills returned

157
00:05:07,321 --> 00:05:09,900
well under one percent after inflation.

158
00:05:10,080 --> 00:05:12,727
The difference compounds into a massive gap.

159
00:05:13,078 --> 00:05:15,458
Of course, stocks come with risk.

160
00:05:15,638 --> 00:05:17,687
They can fall sharply and stay down

161
00:05:17,687 --> 00:05:18,273
for years.

162
00:05:18,453 --> 00:05:20,367
That's why cash still has a role

163
00:05:20,367 --> 00:05:21,623
for short term needs.

164
00:05:21,803 --> 00:05:24,013
But for money you won't touch for

165
00:05:24,013 --> 00:05:26,893
decades, cash's safety comes at an enormous

166
00:05:26,893 --> 00:05:27,898
long term cost.

167
00:05:28,248 --> 00:05:30,001
Here's another way to see the gap.

168
00:05:30,181 --> 00:05:31,435
In the cash example,

169
00:05:31,435 --> 00:05:33,692
every dollar of the final balance is

170
00:05:33,692 --> 00:05:35,510
a dollar you put in yourself.

171
00:05:35,690 --> 00:05:37,231
In the invested example,

172
00:05:37,231 --> 00:05:39,287
more than four out of every five

173
00:05:39,287 --> 00:05:41,600
dollars at the end came from growth,

174
00:05:41,600 --> 00:05:43,399
not from your contributions.

175
00:05:43,579 --> 00:05:45,866
Investing doesn't ask you to work harder.

176
00:05:46,046 --> 00:05:47,938
It asks your money to work too.

177
00:05:48,289 --> 00:05:49,078
Try this.

178
00:05:49,258 --> 00:05:51,556
Calculate how much you've saved in cash

179
00:05:51,556 --> 00:05:53,147
beyond your emergency fund.

180
00:05:53,327 --> 00:05:55,827
Then use a compound interest calculator to

181
00:05:55,827 --> 00:05:57,910
see what that money might become if

182
00:05:57,910 --> 00:05:59,457
invested for twenty years.

183
00:05:59,808 --> 00:06:01,131
Lesson number three.

184
00:06:01,311 --> 00:06:02,980
There's a ceiling on how much you

185
00:06:02,980 --> 00:06:03,436
can save.

186
00:06:03,786 --> 00:06:05,871
Saving comes from the gap between what

187
00:06:05,871 --> 00:06:07,408
you earn and what you spend.

188
00:06:07,588 --> 00:06:09,057
You can cut spending,

189
00:06:09,057 --> 00:06:10,177
but only so far.

190
00:06:10,357 --> 00:06:13,765
Rent, food, transportation and insurance have a

191
00:06:13,765 --> 00:06:14,200
floor.

192
00:06:14,380 --> 00:06:16,962
For someone earning fifty thousand dollars a

193
00:06:16,962 --> 00:06:19,721
year, even an aggressive twenty percent savings

194
00:06:19,721 --> 00:06:21,833
rate is ten thousand dollars a year.

195
00:06:22,184 --> 00:06:23,590
Income, on the other hand,

196
00:06:23,590 --> 00:06:24,726
has no fixed ceiling.

197
00:06:24,906 --> 00:06:26,349
A raise, a promotion,

198
00:06:26,349 --> 00:06:27,036
a new job,

199
00:06:27,036 --> 00:06:29,304
a side business or a skill that's

200
00:06:29,304 --> 00:06:31,777
in demand can increase what you earn

201
00:06:31,777 --> 00:06:34,320
far more than cutting can reduce what

202
00:06:34,320 --> 00:06:35,007
you spend.

203
00:06:35,357 --> 00:06:38,508
Personal finance writer Ramit Sethi often makes

204
00:06:38,508 --> 00:06:39,246
this point.

205
00:06:39,426 --> 00:06:41,006
There's a limit to how much you

206
00:06:41,006 --> 00:06:42,433
can cut, but no limit to how

207
00:06:42,433 --> 00:06:43,350
much you can earn.

208
00:06:43,530 --> 00:06:45,336
Cutting spending is important,

209
00:06:45,336 --> 00:06:47,443
but it eventually runs out of room.

210
00:06:47,793 --> 00:06:50,091
This doesn't mean frugality is useless.

211
00:06:50,271 --> 00:06:53,112
It means frugality works best combined with

212
00:06:53,112 --> 00:06:56,018
growing income and investing the difference.

213
00:06:56,369 --> 00:06:58,145
Here's a simple comparison.

214
00:06:58,325 --> 00:07:01,159
Cutting a fifteen dollar streaming service saves

215
00:07:01,159 --> 00:07:03,166
one hundred eighty dollars a year.

216
00:07:03,346 --> 00:07:05,639
Negotiating a three percent raise on a

217
00:07:05,639 --> 00:07:08,414
fifty thousand dollar salary adds one thousand

218
00:07:08,414 --> 00:07:10,103
five hundred dollars a year,

219
00:07:10,103 --> 00:07:12,456
and that raise often carries into every

220
00:07:12,456 --> 00:07:13,482
future raise too.

221
00:07:13,662 --> 00:07:15,005
Both are worth doing,

222
00:07:15,005 --> 00:07:16,924
but they aren't the same size.

223
00:07:17,275 --> 00:07:18,076
Try this.

224
00:07:18,256 --> 00:07:20,765
Write down your biggest three expenses and

225
00:07:20,765 --> 00:07:21,959
your current income.

226
00:07:22,139 --> 00:07:24,278
Ask which has more room to change

227
00:07:24,278 --> 00:07:25,704
in the next two years.

228
00:07:26,054 --> 00:07:27,354
Lesson number four.

229
00:07:27,534 --> 00:07:30,088
Interest is taxed as ordinary income.

230
00:07:30,438 --> 00:07:32,529
Interest from a savings account,

231
00:07:32,529 --> 00:07:34,881
CD or money market fund is generally

232
00:07:34,881 --> 00:07:37,495
taxed as ordinary income at your regular

233
00:07:37,495 --> 00:07:37,822
rate.

234
00:07:38,002 --> 00:07:40,175
If you're in the twenty two percent

235
00:07:40,175 --> 00:07:43,092
federal bracket and earn four percent interest,

236
00:07:43,092 --> 00:07:45,576
you keep roughly three point one percent

237
00:07:45,576 --> 00:07:46,755
before state taxes.

238
00:07:47,106 --> 00:07:48,766
Now subtract inflation.

239
00:07:48,946 --> 00:07:51,150
If inflation is three percent,

240
00:07:51,150 --> 00:07:52,253
your after tax,

241
00:07:52,253 --> 00:07:55,192
after inflation return is close to zero.

242
00:07:55,372 --> 00:07:57,729
Your savings are treading water at best.

243
00:07:58,079 --> 00:08:01,919
Investments held in tax advantaged accounts like

244
00:08:01,919 --> 00:08:03,119
four oh one ks,

245
00:08:03,119 --> 00:08:06,079
IRAs and Roth IRAs avoid yearly taxes

246
00:08:06,079 --> 00:08:06,879
on growth.

247
00:08:07,059 --> 00:08:09,811
And long term capital gains and qualified

248
00:08:09,811 --> 00:08:12,832
dividends in regular accounts often get lower

249
00:08:12,832 --> 00:08:14,443
tax rates than interest.

250
00:08:14,793 --> 00:08:16,121
So the same dollar,

251
00:08:16,121 --> 00:08:18,219
saved in cash or invested in a

252
00:08:18,219 --> 00:08:19,828
tax advantaged account,

253
00:08:19,828 --> 00:08:22,975
can have very different results after decades

254
00:08:22,975 --> 00:08:23,605
of taxes.

255
00:08:23,955 --> 00:08:26,086
This doesn't mean you should avoid interest

256
00:08:26,086 --> 00:08:26,532
entirely.

257
00:08:26,712 --> 00:08:29,451
Your emergency fund still belongs in safe,

258
00:08:29,451 --> 00:08:31,147
interest earning accounts.

259
00:08:31,327 --> 00:08:33,524
It just means that for long term

260
00:08:33,524 --> 00:08:35,859
money, the tax drag on interest is

261
00:08:35,859 --> 00:08:38,536
one more reason cash struggles to build

262
00:08:38,536 --> 00:08:40,871
wealth, and one more reason to use

263
00:08:40,871 --> 00:08:43,274
tax advantaged accounts for growth.

264
00:08:43,624 --> 00:08:44,251
Try this.

265
00:08:44,431 --> 00:08:46,409
Look at last year's tax forms for

266
00:08:46,409 --> 00:08:47,368
interest income.

267
00:08:47,548 --> 00:08:49,361
That's money you paid tax on that

268
00:08:49,361 --> 00:08:51,229
might have grown tax deferred in a

269
00:08:51,229 --> 00:08:52,273
retirement account.

270
00:08:52,623 --> 00:08:53,969
Lesson number five.

271
00:08:54,149 --> 00:08:56,015
Cash has an important job,

272
00:08:56,015 --> 00:08:58,168
just not the job of making you

273
00:08:58,168 --> 00:08:58,526
rich.

274
00:08:58,877 --> 00:09:00,119
Let's be fair to saving.

275
00:09:00,299 --> 00:09:01,669
Cash is essential.

276
00:09:01,849 --> 00:09:04,202
An emergency fund of three to six

277
00:09:04,202 --> 00:09:07,483
months of essential expenses protects you from

278
00:09:07,483 --> 00:09:10,336
job loss, medical bills and car repairs.

279
00:09:10,516 --> 00:09:12,810
It stops a bad month from becoming

280
00:09:12,810 --> 00:09:13,755
a debt spiral.

281
00:09:14,105 --> 00:09:16,603
Cash is also right for short term

282
00:09:16,603 --> 00:09:18,647
goals, like a car purchase,

283
00:09:18,647 --> 00:09:20,994
a down payment in the next year

284
00:09:20,994 --> 00:09:22,963
or two, or a planned trip.

285
00:09:23,143 --> 00:09:25,511
Money you'll need soon shouldn't be exposed

286
00:09:25,511 --> 00:09:26,777
to stock market swings.

287
00:09:27,127 --> 00:09:29,182
The problem isn't having cash.

288
00:09:29,362 --> 00:09:31,469
It's having too much cash for too

289
00:09:31,469 --> 00:09:31,788
long.

290
00:09:31,968 --> 00:09:34,751
Money beyond your emergency fund and near

291
00:09:34,751 --> 00:09:37,263
term goals often sits idle for years,

292
00:09:37,263 --> 00:09:38,621
losing buying power.

293
00:09:38,971 --> 00:09:41,404
A useful mental model is to give

294
00:09:41,404 --> 00:09:42,848
every dollar a job.

295
00:09:43,028 --> 00:09:45,304
Safety dollars stay in cash.

296
00:09:45,484 --> 00:09:48,305
Goal dollars wait in savings or CDs.

297
00:09:48,485 --> 00:09:50,714
Growth dollars get invested for the long

298
00:09:50,714 --> 00:09:50,993
term.

299
00:09:51,343 --> 00:09:53,383
Some people find it helps to see

300
00:09:53,383 --> 00:09:54,531
cash as insurance,

301
00:09:54,531 --> 00:09:55,870
not as an investment.

302
00:09:56,050 --> 00:09:58,193
You don't buy car insurance expecting it

303
00:09:58,193 --> 00:09:59,104
to make you rich.

304
00:09:59,284 --> 00:10:00,799
You buy it so a bad day

305
00:10:00,799 --> 00:10:01,919
doesn't ruin you.

306
00:10:02,099 --> 00:10:04,375
An emergency fund works the same way.

307
00:10:04,555 --> 00:10:06,524
Its return is peace of mind,

308
00:10:06,524 --> 00:10:08,493
and that's worth paying for,

309
00:10:08,493 --> 00:10:09,478
up to a point.

310
00:10:09,828 --> 00:10:10,547
Try this.

311
00:10:10,727 --> 00:10:13,305
Split your savings into three labels,

312
00:10:13,305 --> 00:10:15,046
safety, goals and growth.

313
00:10:15,226 --> 00:10:17,612
Any growth money still sitting in cash

314
00:10:17,612 --> 00:10:19,685
is probably ready to be invested.

315
00:10:20,035 --> 00:10:21,381
Lesson number six.

316
00:10:21,561 --> 00:10:23,576
Time multiplies investing,

317
00:10:23,576 --> 00:10:24,429
not saving.

318
00:10:24,779 --> 00:10:26,199
In a savings account,

319
00:10:26,199 --> 00:10:27,416
time barely helps,

320
00:10:27,416 --> 00:10:29,039
because the rate is low.

321
00:10:29,219 --> 00:10:30,818
In an investment account,

322
00:10:30,818 --> 00:10:33,376
time is the biggest multiplier there is.

323
00:10:33,556 --> 00:10:36,248
Every extra year gives compounding more room

324
00:10:36,248 --> 00:10:36,737
to work.

325
00:10:37,087 --> 00:10:39,612
Three hundred dollars a month for thirty

326
00:10:39,612 --> 00:10:41,190
five years, kept in cash,

327
00:10:41,190 --> 00:10:44,157
totals one hundred twenty six thousand dollars.

328
00:10:44,337 --> 00:10:46,813
Invested at a seven percent average yearly

329
00:10:46,813 --> 00:10:48,995
return, it could grow to roughly five

330
00:10:48,995 --> 00:10:50,351
hundred forty thousand.

331
00:10:50,701 --> 00:10:52,853
That means the longer you leave long

332
00:10:52,853 --> 00:10:53,990
term money in cash,

333
00:10:53,990 --> 00:10:55,066
the more you lose.

334
00:10:55,246 --> 00:10:57,825
A decade of saving without investing can

335
00:10:57,825 --> 00:11:00,210
cost far more than the decade itself,

336
00:11:00,210 --> 00:11:03,176
because those dollars miss their most powerful

337
00:11:03,176 --> 00:11:04,337
compounding years.

338
00:11:04,687 --> 00:11:06,680
The good news is that it's never

339
00:11:06,680 --> 00:11:08,425
too late to start investing.

340
00:11:08,605 --> 00:11:10,684
But the sooner long term money starts

341
00:11:10,684 --> 00:11:12,595
working, the less you need to save

342
00:11:12,595 --> 00:11:13,887
to reach the same goal.

343
00:11:14,238 --> 00:11:15,856
Here's a striking way to think about

344
00:11:15,856 --> 00:11:15,991
it.

345
00:11:16,171 --> 00:11:18,065
For a twenty five year old,

346
00:11:18,065 --> 00:11:21,081
each dollar invested at seven percent could

347
00:11:21,081 --> 00:11:24,098
become roughly fifteen dollars by age sixty

348
00:11:24,098 --> 00:11:24,449
five.

349
00:11:24,629 --> 00:11:26,357
For a forty five year old,

350
00:11:26,357 --> 00:11:29,017
the same dollar might become about four.

351
00:11:29,197 --> 00:11:31,992
Your earliest dollars are your most powerful

352
00:11:31,992 --> 00:11:34,469
ones, which is exactly why leaving them

353
00:11:34,469 --> 00:11:35,803
in cash is so costly.

354
00:11:36,154 --> 00:11:37,094
Try this.

355
00:11:37,274 --> 00:11:40,077
If you're saving for retirement in cash,

356
00:11:40,077 --> 00:11:43,020
move it into a retirement account invested

357
00:11:43,020 --> 00:11:44,001
in a low cost,

358
00:11:44,001 --> 00:11:45,192
diversified fund.

359
00:11:45,542 --> 00:11:46,923
Lesson number seven.

360
00:11:47,103 --> 00:11:49,953
A raise invested beats a lifetime of

361
00:11:49,953 --> 00:11:50,586
coupons.

362
00:11:50,936 --> 00:11:53,152
Imagine you get a ten thousand dollar

363
00:11:53,152 --> 00:11:54,769
raise and invest all of it,

364
00:11:54,769 --> 00:11:57,284
about eight hundred thirty three dollars a

365
00:11:57,284 --> 00:11:59,321
month, for thirty years at a seven

366
00:11:59,321 --> 00:12:01,118
percent average yearly return.

367
00:12:01,298 --> 00:12:03,580
That could grow to roughly one million

368
00:12:03,580 --> 00:12:04,061
dollars.

369
00:12:04,411 --> 00:12:05,890
To match that by cutting,

370
00:12:05,890 --> 00:12:08,139
you'd need to cut eight hundred thirty

371
00:12:08,139 --> 00:12:10,565
three dollars a month from your spending,

372
00:12:10,565 --> 00:12:12,340
every month, for thirty years.

373
00:12:12,520 --> 00:12:13,445
For most people,

374
00:12:13,445 --> 00:12:15,527
that's far harder than earning more.

375
00:12:15,878 --> 00:12:17,541
Research on job changes,

376
00:12:17,541 --> 00:12:19,898
such as wage data from the Federal

377
00:12:19,898 --> 00:12:21,561
Reserve Bank of Atlanta,

378
00:12:21,561 --> 00:12:24,126
has shown that people who switch jobs

379
00:12:24,126 --> 00:12:26,829
have often seen faster wage growth than

380
00:12:26,829 --> 00:12:27,869
those who stay,

381
00:12:27,869 --> 00:12:29,879
at least during many periods.

382
00:12:30,230 --> 00:12:34,540
Skills, certifications, negotiation and career moves can

383
00:12:34,540 --> 00:12:36,464
raise income for decades.

384
00:12:36,644 --> 00:12:39,180
Every raise you invest builds wealth far

385
00:12:39,180 --> 00:12:40,766
faster than saving alone.

386
00:12:41,116 --> 00:12:44,006
Earning more also makes saving easier emotionally.

387
00:12:44,186 --> 00:12:46,362
When your income rises and you invest

388
00:12:46,362 --> 00:12:47,479
part of each raise,

389
00:12:47,479 --> 00:12:49,949
you can save more without cutting anything

390
00:12:49,949 --> 00:12:50,537
you enjoy.

391
00:12:50,717 --> 00:12:53,164
That's far more sustainable than squeezing an

392
00:12:53,164 --> 00:12:55,175
already tight budget year after year.

393
00:12:55,526 --> 00:12:56,303
Try this.

394
00:12:56,483 --> 00:12:58,504
Research the market pay for your role.

395
00:12:58,684 --> 00:12:59,993
If you're below it,

396
00:12:59,993 --> 00:13:01,853
prepare a case for a raise,

397
00:13:01,853 --> 00:13:03,989
or explore other opportunities.

398
00:13:04,340 --> 00:13:05,268
That's angle one.

399
00:13:05,448 --> 00:13:07,440
Inflation, the growth gap,

400
00:13:07,440 --> 00:13:08,972
the savings ceiling,

401
00:13:08,972 --> 00:13:10,351
taxes on interest,

402
00:13:10,351 --> 00:13:11,577
cash's real job,

403
00:13:11,577 --> 00:13:13,262
time and earning more.

404
00:13:13,442 --> 00:13:14,533
Now angle two.

405
00:13:14,713 --> 00:13:15,758
The psychology.

406
00:13:16,108 --> 00:13:17,640
If the math is so clear,

407
00:13:17,640 --> 00:13:19,683
why do so many people keep their

408
00:13:19,683 --> 00:13:20,577
money in cash?

409
00:13:20,757 --> 00:13:23,788
Because the psychology of saving is powerful.

410
00:13:23,968 --> 00:13:25,192
Cash feels safe,

411
00:13:25,192 --> 00:13:26,875
investing feels risky,

412
00:13:26,875 --> 00:13:29,400
and our brains are wired to avoid

413
00:13:29,400 --> 00:13:29,935
losses.

414
00:13:30,115 --> 00:13:33,163
Here are seven psychological reasons people get

415
00:13:33,163 --> 00:13:34,201
stuck as savers,

416
00:13:34,201 --> 00:13:35,758
and how to move forward.

417
00:13:36,108 --> 00:13:37,385
Lesson number eight.

418
00:13:37,565 --> 00:13:38,698
Cash feels safe,

419
00:13:38,698 --> 00:13:40,328
but it isn't risk free.

420
00:13:40,678 --> 00:13:42,999
The number in a savings account never

421
00:13:42,999 --> 00:13:43,627
goes down.

422
00:13:43,807 --> 00:13:45,966
That stability feels like safety.

423
00:13:46,146 --> 00:13:48,155
But inflation is a risk too.

424
00:13:48,335 --> 00:13:50,222
It's just slow and invisible,

425
00:13:50,222 --> 00:13:52,501
so it doesn't trigger the same fear

426
00:13:52,501 --> 00:13:54,128
as a falling stock price.

427
00:13:54,478 --> 00:13:57,649
Economists sometimes call this money illusion,

428
00:13:57,649 --> 00:14:00,408
the tendency to think in nominal dollars

429
00:14:00,408 --> 00:14:02,407
instead of real buying power.

430
00:14:02,587 --> 00:14:05,172
A balance of ten thousand dollars feels

431
00:14:05,172 --> 00:14:07,492
the same today and in twenty years,

432
00:14:07,492 --> 00:14:09,878
even though it buys much less later.

433
00:14:10,229 --> 00:14:13,085
Long term, the biggest financial risk for

434
00:14:13,085 --> 00:14:15,383
many savers isn't a market crash.

435
00:14:15,563 --> 00:14:17,742
It's running out of money in retirement,

436
00:14:17,742 --> 00:14:19,975
or never building enough to have options.

437
00:14:20,326 --> 00:14:21,917
Recognizing both risks,

438
00:14:21,917 --> 00:14:24,063
market risk and inflation risk,

439
00:14:24,063 --> 00:14:25,655
helps you balance them,

440
00:14:25,655 --> 00:14:28,215
instead of only avoiding the one that

441
00:14:28,215 --> 00:14:29,184
feels scarier.

442
00:14:29,534 --> 00:14:31,494
There's also a risk in being too

443
00:14:31,494 --> 00:14:33,760
cautious with your career and income.

444
00:14:33,940 --> 00:14:35,575
Never asking for a raise,

445
00:14:35,575 --> 00:14:38,258
never learning new skills or never taking

446
00:14:38,258 --> 00:14:40,090
a chance on a better job can

447
00:14:40,090 --> 00:14:42,511
quietly cost far more over a lifetime

448
00:14:42,511 --> 00:14:43,820
than a market crash.

449
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:46,235
Playing it safe isn't always the safe

450
00:14:46,235 --> 00:14:46,658
choice.

451
00:14:47,009 --> 00:14:47,821
Try this.

452
00:14:48,001 --> 00:14:50,325
Ask yourself which worries you more,

453
00:14:50,325 --> 00:14:52,714
a temporary market drop or not having

454
00:14:52,714 --> 00:14:53,876
enough at seventy.

455
00:14:54,056 --> 00:14:55,853
Your answer can guide how much you

456
00:14:55,853 --> 00:14:56,541
keep in cash.

457
00:14:56,891 --> 00:14:58,110
Lesson number nine.

458
00:14:58,290 --> 00:15:00,681
Frugality fatigue is real.

459
00:15:01,032 --> 00:15:02,802
Some people try to get rich by

460
00:15:02,802 --> 00:15:04,631
cutting every possible expense.

461
00:15:04,811 --> 00:15:07,322
No vacations, no dinners out,

462
00:15:07,322 --> 00:15:08,967
no small pleasures.

463
00:15:09,147 --> 00:15:10,642
It can work for a while,

464
00:15:10,642 --> 00:15:12,199
but many people burn out,

465
00:15:12,199 --> 00:15:14,441
and then swing back to overspending.

466
00:15:14,792 --> 00:15:17,507
Extreme frugality can also take time and

467
00:15:17,507 --> 00:15:20,222
energy away from things that grow wealth

468
00:15:20,222 --> 00:15:22,190
faster, like building skills,

469
00:15:22,190 --> 00:15:24,498
networking or starting a business.

470
00:15:24,678 --> 00:15:26,797
Spending hours to save a few dollars

471
00:15:26,797 --> 00:15:27,975
can be a poor trade.

472
00:15:28,325 --> 00:15:30,637
A more sustainable approach is to cut

473
00:15:30,637 --> 00:15:33,261
ruthlessly on things you don't care about,

474
00:15:33,261 --> 00:15:35,573
and spend on things that truly matter

475
00:15:35,573 --> 00:15:36,010
to you.

476
00:15:36,190 --> 00:15:39,093
That keeps saving sustainable for decades.

477
00:15:39,443 --> 00:15:41,823
Being a great saver is a strength.

478
00:15:42,003 --> 00:15:44,114
The trap is believing saving is the

479
00:15:44,114 --> 00:15:44,777
only lever.

480
00:15:44,957 --> 00:15:48,053
The most successful wealth builders pull several

481
00:15:48,053 --> 00:15:49,021
levers at once.

482
00:15:49,371 --> 00:15:52,521
Ramit Sethi calls this spending extravagantly on

483
00:15:52,521 --> 00:15:54,949
the things you love and cutting costs

484
00:15:54,949 --> 00:15:57,312
mercilessly on the things you don't.

485
00:15:57,492 --> 00:15:59,782
It turns saving from a punishment into

486
00:15:59,782 --> 00:16:02,013
a choice, and choices are much easier

487
00:16:02,013 --> 00:16:04,062
to stick with than rules that feel

488
00:16:04,062 --> 00:16:05,027
like punishment.

489
00:16:05,377 --> 00:16:06,154
Try this.

490
00:16:06,334 --> 00:16:08,643
List three expenses you'd happily cut and

491
00:16:08,643 --> 00:16:10,108
three you'd never give up.

492
00:16:10,288 --> 00:16:11,548
Cut the first three,

493
00:16:11,548 --> 00:16:12,934
keep the second three,

494
00:16:12,934 --> 00:16:14,572
and invest the difference.

495
00:16:14,922 --> 00:16:16,083
Lesson number ten.

496
00:16:16,263 --> 00:16:19,097
Fear of losing money keeps people in

497
00:16:19,097 --> 00:16:19,490
cash.

498
00:16:19,840 --> 00:16:23,457
Psychologists Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky found

499
00:16:23,457 --> 00:16:25,961
that losses tend to feel about twice

500
00:16:25,961 --> 00:16:28,466
as painful as equal gains feel good.

501
00:16:28,646 --> 00:16:30,002
That makes investing,

502
00:16:30,002 --> 00:16:31,616
where losses are visible,

503
00:16:31,616 --> 00:16:33,940
feel much scarier than keeping cash.

504
00:16:34,290 --> 00:16:36,852
Many people who lived through crashes,

505
00:16:36,852 --> 00:16:39,213
like 2008, kept their money in cash

506
00:16:39,213 --> 00:16:40,562
for years afterward,

507
00:16:40,562 --> 00:16:43,461
missing much of the recovery that followed.

508
00:16:43,812 --> 00:16:45,522
One way to reduce fear is to

509
00:16:45,522 --> 00:16:47,783
start small and invest automatically,

510
00:16:47,783 --> 00:16:49,982
so you're not making a big emotional

511
00:16:49,982 --> 00:16:51,265
decision all at once.

512
00:16:51,445 --> 00:16:54,226
Another is choosing a balanced fund with

513
00:16:54,226 --> 00:16:55,756
both stocks and bonds,

514
00:16:55,756 --> 00:16:57,564
which tends to swing less.

515
00:16:57,914 --> 00:17:00,578
Over time, as you see your investments

516
00:17:00,578 --> 00:17:01,841
recover from dips,

517
00:17:01,841 --> 00:17:03,173
fear usually fades.

518
00:17:03,353 --> 00:17:05,501
Experience builds confidence.

519
00:17:05,851 --> 00:17:07,499
It also helps to zoom out.

520
00:17:07,679 --> 00:17:08,906
Over a single day,

521
00:17:08,906 --> 00:17:11,290
the stock market falls quite often.

522
00:17:11,470 --> 00:17:12,618
Over a single year,

523
00:17:12,618 --> 00:17:14,490
it has risen more often than it

524
00:17:14,490 --> 00:17:15,940
has fallen historically.

525
00:17:16,120 --> 00:17:17,801
Over twenty year periods,

526
00:17:17,801 --> 00:17:19,684
the S and P five hundred has

527
00:17:19,684 --> 00:17:22,777
historically delivered positive total returns,

528
00:17:22,777 --> 00:17:25,802
though the past doesn't guarantee the future.

529
00:17:25,982 --> 00:17:27,741
The longer your time horizon,

530
00:17:27,741 --> 00:17:30,288
the less frightening the short term swings

531
00:17:30,288 --> 00:17:30,592
look.

532
00:17:30,942 --> 00:17:31,743
Try this.

533
00:17:31,923 --> 00:17:34,987
Invest a small amount automatically each month

534
00:17:34,987 --> 00:17:35,853
for one year,

535
00:17:35,853 --> 00:17:38,517
and watch what happens without reacting.

536
00:17:38,697 --> 00:17:40,706
Then decide whether to increase it.

537
00:17:41,056 --> 00:17:42,263
Quick pause.

538
00:17:42,443 --> 00:17:44,367
If this is changing how you think

539
00:17:44,367 --> 00:17:45,475
about your savings,

540
00:17:45,475 --> 00:17:47,633
please subscribe to Ryan Rover Money.

541
00:17:47,813 --> 00:17:49,424
It's free, and it helps us keep

542
00:17:49,424 --> 00:17:50,100
making these.

543
00:17:50,280 --> 00:17:51,940
Now, lesson eleven.

544
00:17:52,290 --> 00:17:53,520
Lesson number eleven.

545
00:17:53,700 --> 00:17:56,231
A scarcity mindset narrows your focus.

546
00:17:56,582 --> 00:18:00,589
Economist Sendhil Mullainathan and psychologist Eldar Shafir

547
00:18:00,589 --> 00:18:02,526
wrote a book called Scarcity.

548
00:18:02,706 --> 00:18:05,017
They argue that when people feel they

549
00:18:05,017 --> 00:18:06,141
don't have enough,

550
00:18:06,141 --> 00:18:07,015
money or time,

551
00:18:07,015 --> 00:18:09,701
their attention narrows to immediate needs,

552
00:18:09,701 --> 00:18:11,762
making long term planning harder.

553
00:18:12,112 --> 00:18:14,622
For savers, a scarcity mindset can show

554
00:18:14,622 --> 00:18:16,682
up as hoarding cash out of fear,

555
00:18:16,682 --> 00:18:19,321
even when there's enough for emergencies.

556
00:18:19,501 --> 00:18:20,434
It feels safe,

557
00:18:20,434 --> 00:18:22,032
but it can block growth.

558
00:18:22,383 --> 00:18:24,929
Building a solid emergency fund first can

559
00:18:24,929 --> 00:18:26,295
help break this cycle.

560
00:18:26,475 --> 00:18:28,574
Once you know emergencies are covered,

561
00:18:28,574 --> 00:18:30,894
it becomes easier to think about investing

562
00:18:30,894 --> 00:18:31,723
for the future.

563
00:18:32,073 --> 00:18:33,547
Reframing helps too.

564
00:18:33,727 --> 00:18:35,643
Investing isn't spending money.

565
00:18:35,823 --> 00:18:37,790
It's moving money from a place where

566
00:18:37,790 --> 00:18:39,429
it shrinks to a place where it

567
00:18:39,429 --> 00:18:39,921
can grow.

568
00:18:40,271 --> 00:18:42,324
Scarcity can also be a phase,

569
00:18:42,324 --> 00:18:43,881
not a permanent state.

570
00:18:44,061 --> 00:18:46,535
Many people who once lived paycheck to

571
00:18:46,535 --> 00:18:48,682
paycheck later build real wealth.

572
00:18:48,862 --> 00:18:50,852
The key is moving step by step,

573
00:18:50,852 --> 00:18:52,777
